 ],
 "literal": "Work indeed is life to men, O bright-foreheaded one! / To home-dwelling women, men are life, saying that, / to us, the one who said is he himself. / Do not weep, friend, he will call off his journey.",
 "translation": "Endeavor is the very life of men, and men are the life of women who dwell at home—so he himself told us once, O bright-browed one. Do not weep, my friend, he will surely call off his journey.",
 "modern": "ஒளிரும் நெற்றியை உடையவளே! ஆண்களுக்கு அவர்கள் செய்யும் தொழிலே உயிர் ஆகும்; வீட்டில் வாழும் பெண்களுக்கு அந்த ஆண்களே உயிர் என்று நமக்குச் சொன்னவர் அவரே தான். தோழியே, நீ அழாதே! அவர் தன் பயணத்தைத் தவிர்த்துவிடுவார்.",
 "explanation": "In this Pālai (separation) poem, the confidante comforts the weeping heroine who is distressed by the hero's impending departure to earn wealth. She reminds the heroine of the hero's own past words: while work is the very life of men, husbands are the life of home-dwelling women. Since he knows this truth so well, the confidante reassures her that he will not bear to leave her in grief and will surely cancel his journey. The poem beautifully contrasts the external duty of a man with the internal emotional world of a woman. Key words include 'வினை' (work), 'உயிர்' (life), and 'அழுங்குவர்' (he will call off).",
